<p><a class="cimage" href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/2025-11-25.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/2025-11-25.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="476" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-118206" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/2025-11-25.jpeg 1370w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/2025-11-25-300x170.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/2025-11-25-1024x581.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/2025-11-25-768x436.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>The other day, I was happily ripping a new bunch of DVDs and blu rays that had arrived (so that I can actually watch them).  Afterwards I pointed Emacs at the new directories to add metadata semi-automatically&#8230;  only to find that the computer just said &#8220;no&#8221;.</p>
<p>Well, the code works by scraping <tt>imdb.com</tt>, and the HTML changes once in a while, so that&#8217;s no big worry.  But looking at what I got, I got pretty puzzled:</p>
<pre>HTTP/2 202 
server: CloudFront
date: Mon, 24 Nov 2025 23:58:24 GMT
content-length: 2388
x-amzn-waf-action: challenge
access-control-expose-headers: x-amzn-waf-action
x-cache: Error from cloudfront
via: 1.1 079d0a29fa76c3721f14a4132ec9e372.cloudfront.net (CloudFront)
x-amz-cf-pop: ARN52-P1
alt-svc: h3=":443"; ma=86400
x-amz-cf-id: Q6mGF4pMTpmxiyQJUsZN5m8RuTD9dsU0xpzpENImJVvn7jRzdLkVqA==</pre>
<p>And that&#8217;s it.  Just a HTTP 202, and some headers.  But that <tt>x-amzn-waf-action</tt>&#8230;  WAF probably doesn&#8217;t mean &#8220;wife acceptance factor&#8221; in this context, right?  So I googled, and this is indeed Amazon&#8217;s way to fend off horrible people like me that just wants to automate fetching the name of the director of a movie without opening a browser.</p>
<p>Sorry, I misspelled &#8220;AI scrapers&#8221;.</p>
<p>Fair enough.  But how does this actually work, and how trivial is it to work around?</p>
<p>Well, it&#8217;s a Javascript challenge.  If you say this:</p>
<pre>curl -D /tmp/h -H "Accept: text/html" 'https://www.imdb.com/find?q=If+Looks+Could+Kill&ref_=hm_nv_srb_sm'</pre>
<p>You get the JS (shown in the image above).  So basically, you have to spin up a more or less complete browser to fetch a web page from IMDB now.  But *type* *type* *type*, et alors:</p>
<p><a class="cimage" href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/2025-11-25-1.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/2025-11-25-1.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="673" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-118207" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/2025-11-25-1.jpeg 1025w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/2025-11-25-1-300x241.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/2025-11-25-1-768x616.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>Now it works again &#8212; I can hit <tt>a</tt> on a newly ripped movie, and Emacs prompts me for likely matches, and I hit <tt>ret</tt>, and:</p>
<p><a class="cimage" href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/2025-11-25-4.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/2025-11-25-4.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="690" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-118217" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/2025-11-25-4.jpeg 1278w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/2025-11-25-4-300x246.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/2025-11-25-4-1024x841.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/2025-11-25-4-768x631.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>There.  Release year, country, director and poster, all scraped.  The only difference is that it now takes two seconds to fetch the data instead of half a second.  That&#8217;s progress for you, I guess.</p>
<p>If you want to look at the resulting trivial code, it&#8217;s on <a href="https://github.com/larsmagne/imdb.el">Microsoft Github</a>.  (It&#8217;s just a trivial Selenium script.)</p>
<p>Like sand through the hourglass so are the days of our lives.</p>
